WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.451
The AWS CLI is one of the most practical
ways to connect Bash automation to AWS

2
00:00:07.531 --> 00:00:08.508
services.

3
00:00:08.588 --> 00:00:15.431
Teams use it to query resources, publish
artifacts, deploy applications,

4
00:00:15.511 --> 00:00:20.724
update configuration, create storage,
change routing,

5
00:00:20.804 --> 00:00:28.190
start compute resources, inspect logs, and
coordinate operational workflows.

6
00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.311
Those activities may be routine,

7
00:00:31.391 --> 00:00:38.776
but they are also security-relevant
because the CLI acts against the AWS control

8
00:00:38.856 --> 00:00:39.508
plane.

9
00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:46.213
Every AWS CLI command runs with the
identity, account, region, profile,

10
00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:49.877
and permissions available at runtime.

11
00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:56.908
That means a small script can have a large
effect if it uses the wrong profile,

12
00:00:56.988 --> 00:01:01.550
assumes the wrong role, targets the wrong
region,

13
00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.865
or passes an unexpected resource
identifier.

14
00:01:05.945 --> 00:01:13.548
A helper that was meant to support a
staging deployment can cause production impact

15
00:01:13.628 --> 00:01:17.212
if its target assumptions are unclear.

16
00:01:17.292 --> 00:01:22.179
AWS CLI security is not only about
blocking mistakes.

17
00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:29.210
It is about designing automation so the
safe path is obvious and repeatable.

18
00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:35.590
A script should know what account it
expects, what region is in scope,

19
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.535
which role it should use, what resources
it is allowed to affect,

20
00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:45.742
and what output later automation will
trust.

21
00:01:45.822 --> 00:01:52.664
When those facts are unknown, the script
should fail before taking action.

22
00:01:52.744 --> 00:01:59.912
The defensive goal is scoped,
least-privileged, observable AWS automation.

23
00:01:59.992 --> 00:02:05.314
That means validating targets, protecting
credentials,

24
00:02:05.394 --> 00:02:11.042
invoking commands predictably, handling
output carefully,

25
00:02:11.122 --> 00:02:17.313
guarding high-impact changes, and leaving
useful audit evidence.

26
00:02:17.393 --> 00:02:19.891
Bash helpers can be simple,

27
00:02:19.971 --> 00:02:27.899
but simple automation still deserves
production-grade security thinking when it can

28
00:02:27.979 --> 00:02:30.260
change cloud resources.
